 "standard_text": "Use the relation i² = −1 and the commutative, associative, and distributive properties to add, subtract, and multiply complex numbers.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra 2 worksheet on complex numbers: square roots of negatives, powers of i, adding, multiplying, dividing with conjugates and complex solutions of quadratics. 100 questions.",
 "key_big": "i² = −1. Combine real parts and imaginary parts separately.",
 "key_sub": "To divide, multiply by the conjugate of the denominator.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>The imaginary unit is <b><i>i</i> = √−1</b>, so <b><i>i</i>² = −1</b>. A <b>complex number</b> is <i>a</i> + <i>bi</i>, with a real part <i>a</i> and an imaginary part <i>b</i>.</p><ul><li>√−<i>n</i> = <i>i</i>√<i>n</i>. Take out the <i>i</i> before multiplying roots.</li><li>Powers of <i>i</i> repeat every 4: <i>i</i>, −1, −<i>i</i>, 1.</li><li>Add and subtract real parts and imaginary parts separately. Multiply with FOIL, then replace <i>i</i>² with −1.</li><li>To divide, multiply the top and bottom by the <b>conjugate</b> of the bottom (<i>a</i> + <i>bi</i> → <i>a</i> − <i>bi</i>).</li></ul><p>If the discriminant <i>b</i>² − 4<i>ac</i> is negative, a quadratic has two nonreal solutions that are conjugates.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify (3 + 2<i>i</i>)(4 − <i>i</i>).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>12 − 3<i>i</i> + 8<i>i</i> − 2<i>i</i>²</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>12 + 5<i>i</i> + 2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 14 + 5<i>i</i></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify 10 ÷ (1 + 3<i>i</i>).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply by (1 − 3<i>i</i>)/(1 − 3<i>i</i>)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>(10 − 30<i>i</i>) ÷ 10</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 1 − 3<i>i</i></p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Writing <i>i</i>² = 1. It is −1.</li><li>Multiplying √−4 · √−9 as √36 = 6. Write 2<i>i</i> · 3<i>i</i> = −6.</li><li>Forgetting to distribute the minus sign when subtracting a complex number.</li><li>Dividing the real and imaginary parts separately. Use the conjugate.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is i in math?",
   "a": "i is the imaginary unit, defined so that i² = −1. It lets us take square roots of negative numbers: √−9 = 3i."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you simplify powers of i?",
   "a": "Divide the exponent by 4 and use the remainder: remainder 0 gives 1, 1 gives i, 2 gives −1, 3 gives −i."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you divide complex numbers?",
   "a": "Multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, so the denominator becomes a real number."
  },
  {
   "q": "When does a quadratic have complex solutions?",
   "a": "When its discriminant b² − 4ac is negative. The two solutions are complex conjugates, a + bi and a − bi."
  }
